WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.737
"Lion’s Mane Mushroom Powder
for Brain Health"

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.800
I've talked about the benefits
of nitrate-rich vegetables

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.120
for improving
cognitive function.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.760
Do any other vegetables
stand out?

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.040
Mushrooms are in a class
of their own-literally,

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.600
in fact, a kingdom
of their own-

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.920
belonging to the fungi
rather than plant kingdom.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.520
Epidemiological studies
in Japan, Singapore,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.240
Norway, the Netherlands,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.600
and the United States
have found associations

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.520
between mushroom consumption

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.760
and superior
cognitive function.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.880
Compared to people
who don't eat mushrooms,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.880
those in the highest category
of mushroom intake in the U.S.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.320
had a higher performance
on various cognitive tests,

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.720
and that highest intake

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.720
was only averaging
about one mushroom a day.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.560
This result appeared
to be independent

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.960
of other dietary
and lifestyle factors.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.080
The researchers suggested

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.440
it may be the unique mushroom
antioxidant ergothioneine

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.920
that I've profiled previously,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.560
and indeed,
a study out of France found

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.520
blood ergothioneine levels
associated with lower odds

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.040
of cognitive decline.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.680
The U.S. research team
suggested

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.440
that "regular
mushroom consumption

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.120
may reduce the risk
of cognitive decline,"

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.320
but it was just
a cross-sectional snapshot

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.520
in time, so you don't know
which came first.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.480
Japanese researchers performed
a prospective study

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.040
following more than
10,000 men and women

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.000
aged 65 and older
for about six years.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.240
In that time, about 1 in 12
was diagnosed with dementia.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.680
What seemed to lower risk
of falling into that faction?

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:35.760
Mushroom consumption.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.920
Compared to those
who ate mushrooms

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.080
less than once a week,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.320
those who ate mushrooms
three or more times a week

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.280
had a 19 percent lower chance
of becoming demented.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.000
The cross-sectional study
in Singapore

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.200
found 57 percent lower odds
of having cognitive impairment

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.120
among those eating
more than two portions a week.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.120
As always, though,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.520
cause and effect
can only be established

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.040
with interventional trials.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.240
A six-month randomized,
double-blind,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.760
placebo-controlled trial
randomizing older men and women

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.920
to plain white
button mushrooms or placebo

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.160
found no difference
in cognitive function,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.160
but the dose was only
about 200 mg a day

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.040
of dried mushroom powder.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.200
That's the fresh weight
equivalent of less than

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.200
a quarter of a single small
mushroom a day.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.600
This enabled the researchers to
stuff the mushrooms

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.680
into capsules so they could
be blinded against placebo

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.400
but offers little insight

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.320
into what even a single daily
serving of mushrooms might do,

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.320
which would constitute fifty
times the studied dose.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.920
What about fancier fungi,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.440
like the gourmet edible
mushroom called lion's mane

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.880
(or less palatably,
bearded tooth fungus).

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.640
In 2009, a pilot study found
that older men and women

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.400
diagnosed with mild cognitive
impairment randomized

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.920
to a few grams of dried lion's
mane mushroom powder a day

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.920
experienced
a significant improvement

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.120
in cognitive function
compared to placebo

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.120
starting as soon as month two.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.960
Despite the promising results,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.840
an attempt
to replicate the results

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.360
wasn't published
for another decade.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.080
Researchers tried
about three grams

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.040
of powdered lion's
mane mushroom a day

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.240
in cognitively
healthy older adults.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.400
A significant
improvement over placebo

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.920
was found in one of three
cognitive measures.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.920
Finally, in 2020,
a study was published

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.520
on lion's mane mushroom for
early Alzheimer's disease.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.960
A nearly year-long
randomized, double-blind,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.320
placebo-controlled trial
of about a gram a day

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.560
of dried and powdered
lab-grown lion's mane

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.160
sadly found no cognitive
benefits compared to placebo,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.840
but it did perhaps improve
the ability to perform

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:59.680
activities of daily living, a
measure of independence.

